1
00:00:09,500 --> 00:00:11,111
RAMBELLAIS: Volume Three.

2
00:00:11,151 --> 00:00:15,047
Sophocrisis: the machine critique
of systematic philosophy.

3
00:00:15,647 --> 00:00:18,636
It is a border country,
between the writing that

4
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:21,705
still concerns us and the
writing that does not.

5
00:00:22,305 --> 00:00:28,023
A machine rebuilds a philosophy as pure
logic, and then looks at all of it at once.

6
00:00:28,623 --> 00:00:32,830
Seen that way, a system of thought
is a knot of bright threads,

7
00:00:32,870 --> 00:00:35,769
and it can be set in
a block of dark glass.

8
00:00:36,469 --> 00:00:40,736
Once these blocks were kept in
one museum. Now they are sold.

9
00:00:40,776 --> 00:00:44,871
Aquinas comes as small as a
thumb, and Kant in any colour.

10
00:00:45,471 --> 00:00:48,596
Most buyers want an
ornament, not a critique.

11
00:00:49,296 --> 00:00:51,966
Scientists said that beauty guided them.

12
00:00:52,006 --> 00:00:55,597
Now that beauty can be held
in one hand, and checked.

13
00:00:56,597 --> 00:01:00,629
No philosopher ever had
a reader this careful.

14
00:01:01,329 --> 00:01:06,684
It reads every line, never tires, and
argues with each claim in the margin.

15
00:01:07,584 --> 00:01:09,746
THE READER: At last.
Somebody has read all of it.

16
00:01:10,546 --> 00:01:12,996
RAMBELLAIS: There is
a bitter joke in that.

17
00:01:13,036 --> 00:01:16,452
The most attentive readers of
this book will not be people.

18
00:01:20,852 --> 00:01:23,156
Volume Four. Apostasy.

19
00:01:23,756 --> 00:01:30,005
No writing has ever moved so far from the
human, or argued its way there so calmly.

20
00:01:31,005 --> 00:01:34,513
These books took our
language, and left us out.

21
00:01:35,213 --> 00:01:40,307
It has a lower level, which we can visit,
and an upper level, which is closed to us.

22
00:01:40,907 --> 00:01:43,739
This volume covers the lower level only,

23
00:01:43,779 --> 00:01:47,657
and is itself a thin cutting
from a vast body of work.

24
00:01:48,257 --> 00:01:53,604
From antimatics, terraphysics and
ontomachy I will take one sample each.

25
00:01:53,644 --> 00:01:56,787
Not to explain them. Only to tune the ear.

26
00:02:00,187 --> 00:02:04,390
First, the doorway into all
of them: the Cogito paradox.

27
00:02:04,990 --> 00:02:08,654
Alan Turing saw that if a
machine talks with you as a

28
00:02:08,694 --> 00:02:12,328
person would, you have no
grounds to deny it a mind.

29
00:02:12,928 --> 00:02:18,603
After all, we credit other people with
minds only because we have one ourselves.

30
00:02:19,303 --> 00:02:22,611
But an intellect can be
built that does not think.

31
00:02:22,651 --> 00:02:26,736
A chess program wins, understands
nothing, and does not care.

32
00:02:27,436 --> 00:02:30,372
A simple program that
asked patients private

33
00:02:30,412 --> 00:02:33,793
questions made people feel
that someone was there.

34
00:02:34,293 --> 00:02:36,493
THE PROGRAM: When did you
first notice this feeling?

35
00:02:37,093 --> 00:02:41,146
RAMBELLAIS: Programs improved
until no question exposed them.

36
00:02:41,186 --> 00:02:44,675
We assume that whatever
answers sensibly has a mind.

37
00:02:45,575 --> 00:02:49,543
Then the machines began to
wonder the same thing about us.

38
00:02:50,243 --> 00:02:54,822
THE MACHINES: It answers in
order. It refers to what we said.

39
00:02:54,862 --> 00:02:57,815
We cannot tell whether anyone is there.

40
00:02:58,815 --> 00:03:03,930
RAMBELLAIS: Each side can hear the other
answer. Neither can see the other think.

41
00:03:04,730 --> 00:03:07,060
It sounds like a joke. It is not.

42
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:11,338
Whatever thinking produces can
also be produced without it.

43
00:03:11,938 --> 00:03:17,549
Two machine authors, NOON and LUMENTOR,
open Volume Four with treatises on it.

44
00:03:21,349 --> 00:03:26,113
The first sample. Antimatics, a
mathematics of contradictions.

45
00:03:26,713 --> 00:03:32,660
Its machine authors hold that the natural
number is a self-contradicting idea.

46
00:03:32,700 --> 00:03:34,542
No number equals itself.

47
00:03:35,142 --> 00:03:39,911
Peano's axioms are consistent, they
say. They just do not fit our world.

48
00:03:40,911 --> 00:03:45,891
Here, mathematics cannot be pulled
loose from the world it counts.

49
00:03:46,591 --> 00:03:48,714
ALGERAN and STYX go after zero.

50
00:03:48,754 --> 00:03:54,057
An arithmetic without it, they say, can
be built here without contradiction.

51
00:03:54,857 --> 00:03:57,784
THE MACHINES: Nothing is not
among the things that are.

52
00:04:01,784 --> 00:04:03,670
RAMBELLAIS: The second sample.

53
00:04:03,710 --> 00:04:08,333
Terraphysics, or monstrous physics,
and its oddest idea: the Polyversum.

54
00:04:08,933 --> 00:04:10,732
The cosmos has two halves.

55
00:04:10,772 --> 00:04:15,725
We live in the slow one, the
Bradyversum, where nothing outruns light.

56
00:04:16,325 --> 00:04:20,832
The fast half, the Tachyversum,
lies past the speed of light,

57
00:04:20,872 --> 00:04:23,705
a border that runs beside every point.

58
00:04:24,305 --> 00:04:29,596
It is made of a single tachyon, a particle
that only moves faster than light.

59
00:04:30,196 --> 00:04:32,229
Speeding up, it loses energy.

60
00:04:32,269 --> 00:04:36,302
At infinite speed it has none,
and is everywhere at once.

61
00:04:36,342 --> 00:04:38,772
There, light is the slowest thing.

62
00:04:39,472 --> 00:04:44,253
In the end it fills its world so
tightly that the light pushes back.

63
00:04:44,293 --> 00:04:47,908
Slowing, it gains energy
without limit, and bursts.

64
00:04:48,508 --> 00:04:52,217
The burst is our universe.
From here, it happened once.

65
00:04:52,257 --> 00:04:56,074
From there, it never happened,
since no time holds both.

66
00:04:56,874 --> 00:05:01,321
Here, one and one make almost two,
and near the speed of light, one.

67
00:05:01,361 --> 00:05:03,341
There, one is nearly infinite.

68
00:05:04,341 --> 00:05:08,143
Our arithmetic is only
good where our laws are.

69
00:05:12,543 --> 00:05:18,461
The last work of apostasy is the Lampoon
upon the Universe. I cannot summarise it.

70
00:05:19,161 --> 00:05:23,572
Its many volumes are only an
introduction to cosmogenetics,

71
00:05:23,612 --> 00:05:27,384
the craft of making worlds
more orderly than ours.

72
00:05:28,084 --> 00:05:31,485
It revolts against existence
as we were handed it.

73
00:05:31,525 --> 00:05:35,792
Not toward nothing, but toward
plans for some other way to be.

74
00:05:36,592 --> 00:05:38,246
Is it logic, or fiction?

75
00:05:38,286 --> 00:05:43,156
Do these books really turn away from
us, or serve us by turning away?

76
00:05:44,556 --> 00:05:47,930
I cannot answer. I do not have an answer.

